; swap -- more processes than core, and two of them sharing one binary's text.
;
; Task 26's "done when", and the fifth test that boots the whole kernel.  Where `session'
; has the shell write files and `libtest' runs the library suite one program at a time,
; this one squeezes the machine until the swapper has to work, and then asks the kernel
; itself whether it did.  kernel/test/swap.sh is the load; run-swap.sh drives the whole
; thing and checks on the host what came back.
;
; GENERATED FROM swap.ini.in by genboot.cmake, because every address below is a link-time
; value that moves as the kernel grows.  Four symbols: `phymem', which is deposited, and
; the three counters, which are asserted.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE PRESSURE KNOB, and why it is a deposit rather than a kernel change.
;
; kernel/machdep.c has `int phymem = 512 * 1024' -- plain initialized data, one reference,
; and startup() derives everything else from it: maxmem, the extent it frees into the
; coremap, and the two banner lines below.  So one `deposit' before `go' models a smaller
; machine exactly, and the kernel that ships is untouched.
;
; It has to be a deposit because the numbers do not otherwise meet.  A full machine frees
; 479 pages of core against 512 pages of swap -- core is very nearly as large as the paging
; store -- so filling core needs about as many processes as there is swap to hold them, and
; the swapper would never run.  @PHYMEM@ = 0200000 is 64 pages, of which the kernel image,
; the buffer cache and the u-area take 33, leaving 31 for user images.
;
; THE BANNER IS THE RECEIPT.  `user mem = 186 kbytes' is 31744 words; the expect below is
; what makes a deposit that silently missed -- a moved symbol, a typo in the address -- a
; failure here rather than a test that quietly runs on a full machine and swaps nothing.
;
; The floor is not far below: estabur() refuses any process bigger than maxmem, and both
; binaries here are pure -- /bin/sh is 4 private pages over a 7-page shared text, puret 4
; over 1 -- so much under 24 pages of core and execs start failing with ENOMEM instead of
; swapping.  If you lower this, watch for `sh: cannot execute' on the console rather than
; for a hang.  (Impure, /bin/sh was ten pages a copy, and this number was chosen then.)
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; THE COUNTERS, and why the `if' below is spelled the way it is.  The parenthesised form
; every other .ini uses -- `if (PC != 0611)' -- goes through SIMH's expression evaluator,
; which knows registers, environment variables and literals and CANNOT name a memory
; address.  The bare form does: `if <addr> <cond>' reads the word (scp.c, get_aval).  What
; it reads is 50 bits wide, though -- the top two are the parity tag mmu_store() wrote, and
; that tag is 0 or 1 depending on which half-word the storing instruction sat in -- so the
; comparison has to mask to 48 bits with `&07777777777777777'.  No space may appear inside
; the condition token.  `ex' prints the value untagged and is right for the diagnostics.
;
; The three counters are what stop this test from passing for the wrong reason.  A load
; test that only checks the machine survived cannot tell a kernel that swapped from one
; that had room to spare, and shared text that was never joined looks exactly like text
; that was copied.  kernel/test/uswap asserts the same counters against known traffic, so
; a counter that counted the wrong thing fails there first.
;
; THE DISK IS WRITABLE and is this test's own copy at volume 3081 (run-swap.sh converts it);
; the drums are this test's own too, since ctest runs the SIMH tests in parallel.

reset
set cpu latin
set mmu cache
set tty25 raw

attach tty25 console
attach md00 root3081.disk
attach -n drum0 swap0.drum
attach -n drum1 swap1.drum
load ../unix

deposit @PHYMEM@ 0200000

; Each `expect' HALTS the simulator and then runs its action, so every action but the last
; has to end with a `step' of its own to set the machine going again.  The last one does not:
; it jumps to the checks below, which run with the machine stopped.  A `goto' rather than
; falling through, because falling through would also be what a run that merely exhausted its
; step budget did, and the whole point of the FAIL block is to tell those two apart.

expect "user mem  = 186 kbytes" echof "swap: core squeezed to 31 pages, as deposited"; step 50000000
expect "# " send "sh /etc/swap\r"; step 50000000
expect "swap done" goto checks

step 50000000

echof "FAIL: swap -- the load never finished.  The last marker above says how far it got: no `user mem' line means the deposit is wrong, no prompt means the boot is, and no `swap done' means the load itself hung or died.  PC:"
ex PC
exit 1

:checks
echof "swap: the load finished"
if @NSWAPOUT@&07777777777777777==0 echof "FAIL: swap -- nothing was ever swapped OUT.  Either the deposit above did not take (the `user mem' line would have failed first), or the load no longer outgrows the coremap.  nswapout:"; ex @NSWAPOUT@; exit 1
if @NSWAPIN@&07777777777777777==0 echof "FAIL: swap -- something went out to the drum and nothing came back, so sched() never found a swapped-out process runnable.  nswapin:"; ex @NSWAPIN@; exit 1
if @NTEXTJOIN@&07777777777777777==0 echof "FAIL: swap -- no second process ever attached to a text already in text[].  Are /bin/sh and /usr/test/puret still linked PURE?  getxfile() forces ux_tsize to 0 for an FMAGIC image and xalloc() returns on its first line, so an impure image shares nothing at all.  ntextjoin:"; ex @NTEXTJOIN@; exit 1

echof "PASS: swap -- more processes than core, swapped out and back, sharing one text"
ex @NSWAPOUT@
ex @NSWAPIN@
ex @NTEXTJOIN@
quit
